## an3.87
an3.87:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, each fortnight over a hundred and fifty training rules come up for recitation, in which gentlemen who desire self-knowledge train. | T: “Monks, this recitation of more than 150 training rules comes every fortnight, in reference to which sons of good families desiring the goal train themselves.
an3.87:1.2 | S: These are all included in the three trainings. | T: There are these three trainings under which all that is gathered.
an3.87:1.3 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.87:1.4 | S: The training in the higher ethics, the higher mind, and the higher wisdom. | T: The training in heightened virtue, the training in heightened mind, the training in heightened discernment.
an3.87:1.5 | S: These are the three trainings that include them all. | T: These are the three trainings under which all that is gathered.
an3.87:2.1 | S: Take the case of a mendicant who has fulfilled their ethics, but has limited immersion and wisdom. | T: “There is the case where a monk is wholly accomplished in virtue, moderately accomplished in concentration, and moderately accomplished in discernment.
an3.87:2.2 | S: They break some lesser and minor training rules, but are restored. | T: With reference to the lesser and minor training rules, he falls into offenses and rehabilitates himself.
an3.87:2.3 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an3.87:2.4 | S: Because I don’t say they’re incapable of that. | T: Because I have not declared that to be a disqualification in these circumstances.
an3.87:2.5 | S: But they’re constant and steady in their precepts regarding the training rules that are fundamental, befitting the spiritual life. They keep the rules they’ve undertaken. | T: But as for the training rules that are basic to the holy life and proper to the holy life, he is one of permanent virtue, one of steadfast virtue. Having undertaken them, he trains in reference to the training rules.
an3.87:2.6 | S: With the ending of three fetters they have at most seven rebirths. | T: “With the ending of (the first) three fetters, he is one who has seven more times at most.
an3.87:2.7 | S: They will transmigrate at most seven times among gods and humans and then make an end of suffering. | T: Having transmigrated and wandered on among devas and human beings, he will put an end to stress.
an3.87:2.8 | S: With the ending of three fetters, they go from family to family. They will transmigrate between two or three families and then make an end of suffering. | T: “(Or) he is one going from good family to good family [i.e., rebirth in the human realm or any of the deva realms]. Having transmigrated and wandered on among two or three good families, he will put an end to stress.
an3.87:2.9 | S: With the ending of three fetters, they’re a one-seeder. They will be reborn just one time in a human existence, then make an end of suffering. | T: “(Or) he is one with one seed. Having arisen only once more in the human realm, he will put an end to stress.
an3.87:2.10 | S: With the ending of three fetters, and the weakening of greed, hate, and delusion, they’re a once-returner. They come back to this world once only, then make an end of suffering. | T: “(Or), with the ending of (the first) three fetters, and with the attenuation of passion, aversion, & delusion, he is a once-returner who—on returning only once more to this world—will put an end to stress.
an3.87:3.1 | S: Take another case of a mendicant who has fulfilled their ethics and immersion, but has limited wisdom. | T: “There is the case where a monk is wholly accomplished in virtue, wholly accomplished in concentration, and moderately accomplished in discernment.
an3.87:3.2 | S: They break some lesser and minor training rules, but are restored. | T: With reference to the lesser and minor training rules, he falls into offenses and rehabilitates himself.
an3.87:3.3 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an3.87:3.4 | S: Because I don’t say they’re incapable of that. | T: Because I have not declared that to be a disqualification in these circumstances.
an3.87:3.5 | S: But they’re constant and steady in their precepts regarding the training rules that are fundamental, befitting the spiritual path. They keep the rules they’ve undertaken. | T: But as for the training rules that are basic to the holy life and proper to the holy life, he is one of permanent virtue, one of steadfast virtue. Having undertaken them, he trains in reference to the training rules.
an3.87:3.6 | S: With the ending of the five lower fetters they head upstream, going to the Akaniṭṭha realm. | T: “With the ending of the five lower fetters, he is one going upstream to the Peerless [the Akaniṭṭha heaven, the highest of the Pure Abodes].
an3.87:3.7 | S: With the ending of the five lower fetters they’re extinguished with extra effort. | T: “(Or), with the ending of the five lower fetters, he is one unbound with fabrication (of exertion).
an3.87:3.8 | S: With the ending of the five lower fetters they’re extinguished without extra effort. | T: “(Or), with the ending of the five lower fetters, he is one unbound without fabrication (of exertion).
an3.87:3.9 | S: With the ending of the five lower fetters they’re extinguished upon landing. | T: “(Or), with the ending of the five lower fetters, he is one unbound on arrival (in a Pure Abode).
an3.87:3.10 | S: With the ending of the five lower fetters they’re extinguished between one life and the next. | T: “(Or), with the ending of the five lower fetters, he is one unbound in between.
an3.87:4.1 | S: Take another case of a mendicant who has fulfilled their ethics, immersion, and wisdom. | T: “There is the case where a monk is wholly accomplished in virtue, wholly accomplished in concentration, wholly accomplished in discernment.
an3.87:4.2 | S: They break some lesser and minor training rules, but are restored. | T: With reference to the lesser and minor training rules, he falls into offenses and rehabilitates himself.
an3.87:4.3 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
an3.87:4.4 | S: Because I don’t say they’re incapable of that. | T: Because I have not declared that to be a disqualification in these circumstances.
an3.87:4.5 | S: But they’re constant and steady in their precepts regarding the training rules that are fundamental, befitting the spiritual path. They keep the rules they’ve undertaken. | T: But as for the training rules that are basic to the holy life and proper to the holy life, he is one of permanent virtue, one of steadfast virtue. Having undertaken them, he trains in reference to the training rules.
an3.87:4.6 | S: They realize the undefiled freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom. And they live having realized it with their own insight in this very life due to the ending of defilements. | T: With the ending of effluents, he dwells in the effluent-free awareness-release and discernment-release, having directly known and realized them for himself right in the here-and-now.
an3.87:5.1 | S: So, mendicants, if you practice partially you succeed partially. If you practice fully you succeed fully. But in any case, these training rules are not barren, I say.” | T: “Those who are partially accomplished attain a part; those who are wholly accomplished, the whole. The training rules, I tell you, are not in vain.”
an3.87:5.2 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.89
an3.89:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, these are the three trainings. | T: “There are these three trainings.
an3.89:1.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.89:1.3 | S: The training in the higher ethics, the higher mind, and the higher wisdom. | T: The training in heightened virtue, the training in heightened mind, the training in heightened discernment.
an3.89:2.1 | S: And what is the training in the higher ethics? | T: “And what is the training in heightened virtue?
an3.89:2.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant is ethical, restrained in the monastic code, conducting themselves well and resorting for alms in suitable places. Seeing danger in the slightest fault, they keep the rules they’ve undertaken. | T: There is the case where a monk is virtuous. He dwells restrained in accordance with the Pāṭimokkha, consummate in his behavior & sphere of activity. He trains himself, having undertaken the training rules, seeing danger in the slightest fault.
an3.89:2.3 | S: This is called the training in the higher ethics. | T: This is called the training in heightened virtue.
an3.89:3.1 | S: And what is the training in the higher mind? | T: “And what is the training in heightened mind?
an3.89:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant, quite secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unskillful qualities, enters and remains in the first absorption … second absorption … third absorption … fourth absorption. | T: There is the case where a monk—quite secluded from sensuality, secluded from unskillful qualities—enters & remains in the first jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by directed thought & evaluation. With the stilling of directed thoughts & evaluations, he enters & remains in the second jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of concentration, unification of awareness free from directed thought & evaluation—internal assurance. With the fading of rapture, he remains equanimous, mindful, & alert, and senses pleasure with the body. He enters & remains in the third jhāna, of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous & mindful, he has a pleasant abiding.’ With the abandoning of pleasure & pain—as with the earlier disappearance of elation & distress—he enters & remains in the fourth jhāna: purity of equanimity & mindfulness, neither pleasure nor pain.
an3.89:3.3 | S: This is called the training in the higher mind. | T: This is called the training in heightened mind.
an3.89:4.1 | S: And what is the training in the higher wisdom? | T: “And what is the training in heightened discernment?
an3.89:4.2 | S: They truly understand: ‘This is suffering’ … ‘This is the origin of suffering’ … ‘This is the cessation of suffering’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering’. | T: There is the case where a monk discerns as it has come to be that ‘This is stress… This is the origination of stress… This is the cessation of stress… This is the path of practice leading to the cessation of stress.’
an3.89:4.3 | S: This is called the training in the higher wisdom. | T: This is called the training in heightened discernment.
an3.89:4.4 | S: These are the three trainings.” | T: “These are the three trainings.”
an3.89:4.5 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.92
an3.92:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, a farmer has three urgent duties. | T: “There are these three urgent duties of a farming householder.
an3.92:1.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.92:1.3 | S: A farmer swiftly makes sure the field is well ploughed and tilled. | T: “There is the case where a farming householder quickly gets his field well-plowed & well-harrowed.
an3.92:1.4 | S: Next they swiftly plant seeds in season. | T: Having quickly gotten his field well-plowed & well-harrowed, he quickly plants the seed.
an3.92:1.5 | S: When the time is right, they swiftly irrigate or drain the field. | T: Having quickly planted the seed, he quickly lets in the water & then lets it out.
an3.92:1.6 | S: These are the three urgent duties of a farmer. | T: “These are the three urgent duties of a farming householder.
an3.92:1.7 | S: That farmer has no special power or ability to say: | T: Now, that farming householder does not have the power or might (to say:)
an3.92:1.8 | S: ‘Let the crops germinate today! Let them flower tomorrow! Let them ripen the day after!’ | T: ‘May my crops spring up today, may the grains appear tomorrow, and may they ripen the next day.’
an3.92:1.9 | S: But there comes a time when that farmer’s crops germinate, flower, and ripen as the seasons change. | T: But when the time has come, the farming householder’s crops spring up, the grains appear, and they ripen.
an3.92:2.1 | S: In the same way, a mendicant has three urgent duties. | T: “In the same way, there are these three urgent duties of a monk.
an3.92:2.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.92:2.3 | S: Undertaking the training in the higher ethics, the higher mind, and the higher wisdom. | T: The undertaking of the training in heightened virtue, the undertaking of the training in heightened mind, the undertaking of the training in heightened discernment.
an3.92:2.4 | S: These are the three urgent duties of a mendicant. | T: These are the three urgent duties of a monk.
an3.92:2.5 | S: That mendicant has no special power or ability to say: | T: Now, that monk does not have the power or might (to say:)
an3.92:2.6 | S: ‘Let my mind be freed from defilements by not grasping today! Or tomorrow! Or the day after!’ | T: ‘May my mind be released from effluents through lack of clinging/sustenance today or tomorrow or the next day.’
an3.92:2.7 | S: But there comes a time—as that mendicant trains in the higher ethics, the higher mind, and the higher wisdom—that their mind is freed from defilements by not grasping. | T: But when the time has come, his mind is released from effluents through lack of clinging/sustenance.
an3.92:3.1 | S: So you should train like this: | T: “Thus, monks, you should train yourselves:
an3.92:3.2 | S: ‘We will have keen enthusiasm for undertaking the training in the higher ethics, the higher mind, and the higher wisdom.’ | T: ‘Strong will be our desire for the undertaking of the training in heightened virtue. Strong will be our desire for the undertaking of the training in heightened mind. Strong will be our desire for the undertaking of the training in heightened discernment.’
an3.92:3.3 | S: That’s how you should train.” | T: That’s how you should train yourselves.”
an3.92:3.4 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.94
an3.94:1.1 | S: “In the autumn, the heavens are sunny and cloudless. And as the sun is rising to the firmament, having dispelled all the darkness of space, it shines and glows and dazzles. | T: “Monks, just as in autumn, when the sky is clear & cloudless, the sun, on ascending the sky, overpowers the space immersed in darkness, shines, blazes, & dazzles;
an3.94:2.1 | S: In the same way, when the stainless, immaculate vision of the teaching arises in a noble disciple, three fetters are given up: | T: in the same way, when the dustless, stainless Dhamma eye arises in a noble disciple, together with its arising three fetters are abandoned by the noble disciple:
an3.94:2.2 | S: substantialist view, doubt, and misapprehension of precepts and observances. | T: self-identification view, doubt, and grasping at habits & practices.
an3.94:3.1 | S: Afterwards they are delivered from two things: covetousness and ill will. | T: Afterwards, when he leaves two qualities—greed & ill will—
an3.94:3.2 | S: Quite secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unskillful qualities, they enter and remain in the first absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of seclusion, while placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: then, quite secluded from sensuality, secluded from unskillful qualities, he enters & remains in the first jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by directed thought & evaluation.
an3.94:3.3 | S: If that noble disciple passed away at that time, they’re bound by no fetter that might return them to this world.” | T: If the noble disciple passes away at that time, there is no fetter bound by which he would return to this world.”
an3.94:3.4 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.95
an3.95:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, these are the three assemblies. | T: “Monks, there are these three assemblies.
an3.95:1.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.95:1.3 | S: An assembly of the best, a divided assembly, and a harmonious assembly. | T: The assembly of the supreme, the factional assembly, the harmonious assembly.
an3.95:2.1 | S: And what is an assembly of the best? | T: “And which is the assembly of the supreme?
an3.95:2.2 | S: An assembly where the senior mendicants are not indulgent or slack, nor are they backsliders. Instead, they take the lead in seclusion, rousing energy for attaining the unattained, achieving the unachieved, and realizing the unrealized. And those who come afterwards follow their example. | T: There is the case where—in whatever assembly the elder monks are not lax or luxurious, have shunned the tossing away of their duties, have taken the lead in seclusion, and have aroused persistence for the attaining of the as-yet-unattained, the reaching of the as-yet-unreached, the realization of the as-yet-unrealized—the next generation follows their example.
an3.95:2.3 | S: They too are not indulgent or slack, nor are they backsliders. Instead, they take the lead in seclusion, rousing energy for attaining the unattained, achieving the unachieved, and realizing the unrealized. | T: They, too, are not lax or luxurious. They shun the tossing away of their duties, they take the lead in seclusion, and they arouse persistence for the attaining of the as-yet-unattained, the reaching of the as-yet-unreached, the realization of the as-yet-unrealized.
an3.95:2.4 | S: This is called an assembly of the best. | T: This is called the assembly of the supreme.
an3.95:3.1 | S: And what is a divided assembly? | T: “And which is the factional assembly?
an3.95:3.2 | S: An assembly where the mendicants fight, quarrel, and dispute, continually wounding each other with barbed words. This is called a divided assembly. | T: There is the case where, in whatever assembly the monks keep arguing, quarreling, & disputing, stabbing one another with weapons of the mouth, that is called the factional assembly.
an3.95:4.1 | S: And what is a harmonious assembly? | T: “And which is the harmonious assembly?
an3.95:4.2 | S: An assembly where the mendicants live in harmony, appreciating each other, without dispute, blending like milk and water, and regarding each other with kindly eyes. This is called a harmonious assembly. | T: There is the case where, in whatever assembly the monks dwell in harmony, on friendly terms, without quarreling, like milk mixed with water, viewing one another with eyes of affection, that is called the harmonious assembly.
an3.95:5.1 | S: When the mendicants live in harmony like this, they brim with much merit. | T: “Now, when the monks dwell in harmony, on friendly terms, without quarreling, like milk mixed with water, viewing one another with eyes of affection, at that time the monks produce much merit,
an3.95:5.2 | S: At that time the mendicants live in a divine dwelling, that is, the heart’s release by rejoicing. | T: at that time they dwell in a Brahmā dwelling, i.e., empathetic joy as an awareness release.
an3.95:5.3 | S: When they’re joyful, rapture springs up. When the mind is full of rapture, the body becomes tranquil. When the body is tranquil, they feel bliss. And when they’re blissful, the mind becomes immersed in samādhi. | T: When one is joyful, rapture is born. In one who is enraptured at heart, the body grows calm. When the body is calm, one feels pleasure. Feeling pleasure, the mind becomes concentrated.
an3.95:6.1 | S: It’s like when the heavens rain heavily on a mountain top, and the water flows downhill to fill the hollows, crevices, and creeks. As they become full, they fill up the pools. The pools fill up the lakes, the lakes fill up the streams, and the streams fill up the rivers. And as the rivers become full, they fill up the ocean. | T: “Just as when the devas pour rain in heavy drops on the upper mountains: The water, flowing down along the slopes, fills the branches of the mountain ravines & gullies. When the branches of the mountain ravines & gullies are full, they fill the little lakes. When the little lakes are full, they fill the large lakes… the little rivers… the large rivers. When the large rivers are full, they fill the ocean.
an3.95:7.1 | S: In the same way, when the mendicants are in harmony, appreciating each other, without dispute, blending like milk and water, and regarding each other with kindly eyes, they brim with much merit. | T: In the same way, when the monks dwell in harmony, on friendly terms, without quarreling, like milk mixed with water, viewing one another with eyes of affection, at that time the monks produce much merit,
an3.95:7.2 | S: At that time the mendicants live in a divine dwelling, that is, the heart’s release by rejoicing. | T: at that time they dwell in a Brahmā dwelling, i.e., empathetic joy as an awareness release.
an3.95:7.3 | S: When they’re joyful, rapture springs up. When the mind is full of rapture, the body becomes tranquil. When the body is tranquil, they feel bliss. And when they’re blissful, the mind becomes immersed in samādhi. | T: When one is joyful, rapture is born. In one who is enraptured at heart, the body grows calm. When the body is calm, one feels pleasure. Feeling pleasure, the mind becomes concentrated.
an3.95:7.4 | S: These are the three assemblies.” | T: “These, monks, are the three assemblies.”
an3.95:7.5 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.96
an3.96:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, a fine royal thoroughbred with three factors is worthy of a king, fit to serve a king, and reckoned as a factor of kingship. | T: “Endowed with three characteristics, a king’s excellent thoroughbred steed is worthy of a king, the wealth of a king, and counts as one of the king’s own limbs.
an3.96:1.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.96:1.3 | S: It’s when a fine royal thoroughbred is beautiful, strong, and fast. | T: There is the case where a king’s excellent thoroughbred steed is consummate in beauty, consummate in strength, and consummate in speed.
