an3.96:1.6 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.96:1.7 | S: It’s when a mendicant is beautiful, strong, and fast. | T: There is the case where a monk is consummate in beauty, consummate in strength, and consummate in speed.
an3.96:2.1 | S: And how is a mendicant beautiful? | T: “And how is a monk consummate in beauty?
an3.96:2.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant is ethical, restrained in the monastic code, conducting themselves well and resorting for alms in suitable places. Seeing danger in the slightest fault, they keep the rules they’ve undertaken. | T: There is the case where a monk is virtuous. He dwells restrained in accordance with the Pāṭimokkha, consummate in his behavior & sphere of activity. He trains himself, having undertaken the training rules, seeing danger in the slightest fault.
an3.96:2.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant is beautiful. | T: This is how a monk is consummate in beauty.
an3.96:3.1 | S: And how is a mendicant strong? | T: “And how is a monk consummate in strength?
an3.96:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant lives with energy roused up for giving up unskillful qualities and embracing skillful qualities. They are strong, staunchly vigorous, not slacking off when it comes to developing skillful qualities. | T: There is the case where a monk keeps his persistence aroused for abandoning unskillful mental qualities and taking on skillful mental qualities. He is steadfast, solid in his effort, not shirking his duties with regard to skillful mental qualities.
an3.96:3.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant is strong. | T: This is how a monk is consummate in strength.
an3.96:4.1 | S: And how is a mendicant fast? | T: “And how is a monk consummate in speed?
an3.96:4.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant truly understands: ‘This is suffering’ … | T: There is the case where a monk discerns as it has come to be that ‘This is stress.’
an3.96:4.3 | S: ‘This is the origin of suffering’ … | T: He discerns as it has come to be that ‘This is the origination of stress.’
an3.96:4.4 | S: ‘This is the cessation of suffering’ … | T: He discerns as it has come to be that ‘This is the cessation of stress.’
an3.96:4.5 | S: ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering’. | T: He discerns as it has come to be that ‘This is the path of practice leading to the cessation of stress.’1
an3.96:4.6 | S: That’s how a mendicant is fast. | T: This is how a monk is consummate in speed.
an3.96:4.7 | S: A mendicant with these three qualities is worthy of offerings dedicated to the gods, worthy of hospitality, worthy of a religious donation, worthy of veneration with cupped palms, and is the supreme field of merit for the world.” | T: “Endowed with these three qualities is a monk deserving of gifts, deserving of hospitality, deserving of offerings, deserving of respect, an unexcelled field of merit for the world.”
an3.96:4.8 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.97
an3.97:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, a fine royal thoroughbred with three factors is worthy of a king, fit to serve a king, and considered a factor of kingship. | T: “Endowed with three characteristics, a king’s excellent thoroughbred steed is worthy of a king, the wealth of a king, and counts as one of the king’s own limbs.
an3.97:1.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.97:1.3 | S: It’s when a fine royal thoroughbred is beautiful, strong, and fast. | T: There is the case where a king’s excellent thoroughbred steed is consummate in beauty, consummate in strength, and consummate in speed.
an3.97:1.4 | S: A fine royal thoroughbred with these three factors is worthy of a king, … | T: Endowed with these three characteristics is a king’s excellent thoroughbred steed worthy of a king, the wealth of a king, and counts as one of the king’s own limbs.
an3.97:1.5 | S: In the same way, a mendicant with three qualities is worthy of offerings dedicated to the gods, worthy of hospitality, worthy of a religious donation, worthy of veneration with cupped palms, and is the supreme field of merit for the world. | T: “In the same way, a monk endowed with these three qualities is deserving of gifts, deserving of hospitality, deserving of offerings, deserving of respect, an unexcelled field of merit for the world.
an3.97:1.6 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.97:1.7 | S: It’s when a mendicant is beautiful, strong, and fast. | T: There is the case where a monk is consummate in beauty, consummate in strength, and consummate in speed.
an3.97:2.1 | S: And how is a mendicant beautiful? | T: “And how is a monk consummate in beauty?
an3.97:2.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant is ethical, restrained in the monastic code, conducting themselves well and resorting for alms in suitable places. Seeing danger in the slightest fault, they keep the rules they’ve undertaken. | T: There is the case where a monk is virtuous. He dwells restrained in accordance with the Pāṭimokkha, consummate in his behavior & sphere of activity. He trains himself, having undertaken the training rules, seeing danger in the slightest fault.
an3.97:2.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant is beautiful. | T: This is how a monk is consummate in beauty.
an3.97:3.1 | S: And how is a mendicant strong? | T: “And how is a monk consummate in strength?
an3.97:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant lives with energy roused up for giving up unskillful qualities and embracing skillful qualities. They are strong, staunchly vigorous, not slacking off when it comes to developing skillful qualities. | T: There is the case where a monk keeps his persistence aroused for abandoning unskillful mental qualities and taking on skillful mental qualities. He is steadfast, solid in his effort, not shirking his duties with regard to skillful mental qualities.
an3.97:3.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant is strong. | T: This is how a monk is consummate in strength.
an3.97:4.1 | S: And how is a mendicant fast? | T: “And how is a monk consummate in speed?
an3.97:4.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant, with the ending of the five lower fetters, is reborn spontaneously. They’re extinguished there, and are not liable to return from that world. | T: There is the case where a monk, with the wasting away of the five lower fetters, is due to arise spontaneously (in the Pure Abodes), there to be totally unbound, destined never again to return from that world.
an3.97:4.3 | S: That’s how a mendicant is fast. | T: This is how a monk is consummate in speed.
an3.97:4.4 | S: A mendicant with these three qualities is worthy of offerings dedicated to the gods, worthy of hospitality, worthy of a religious donation, worthy of veneration with cupped palms, and is the supreme field of merit for the world.” | T: “Endowed with these three qualities a monk is deserving of gifts, deserving of hospitality, deserving of offerings, deserving of respect, an unexcelled field of merit for the world.”
an3.97:4.5 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

