## an3.99
an3.99:1.1 | S: “Jute canvas is ugly, unpleasant to touch, and worthless whether it’s new, worn in, or worn out. | T: “Monks, a new piece of bark-fiber cloth is ugly, rough to the touch, and of next-to-no value. A piece of bark-fiber cloth of middle standing [in terms of its age] is ugly, rough to the touch, and of next-to-no value. An old piece of bark-fiber cloth is ugly, rough to the touch, and of next-to-no value.
an3.99:1.2 | P: majjhimopi, bhikkhave, potthako dubbaṇṇo ca hoti dukkhasamphasso ca appaggho ca; | T: (none)
an3.99:1.3 | P: jiṇṇopi, bhikkhave, potthako dubbaṇṇo ca hoti dukkhasamphasso ca appaggho ca. | T: (none)
an3.99:1.4 | S: They use worn out jute canvas for scrubbing cauldrons, or else they just throw it away on the rubbish heap. | T: People use an old piece of bark-fiber cloth to scrub pots or they throw it away on a trash heap.
an3.99:2.1 | S: In the same way, if a junior mendicant is unethical, of bad character, | T: “In the same way, monks, when a new monk is unvirtuous & of bad character,
an3.99:2.2 | S: this is how they’re ugly, I say. | T: that, I tell you, is his ugliness.
an3.99:2.3 | S: That individual is just as ugly as jute canvas. | T: Just as a piece of bark-fiber cloth is ugly, this person, I tell you, is like that.
an3.99:2.4 | S: If you associate with, accompany, and attend to them, following their example, it’ll be for your lasting harm and suffering. | T: “For those who associate with him, resort to him, eat with him, attend to him, & follow his example, that will be for their long-term harm & suffering.
an3.99:2.5 | S: This is how they’re unpleasant to touch, I say. | T: That, I tell you, is his being rough to the touch.
an3.99:2.6 | S: That individual is just as unpleasant to touch as jute canvas. | T: Just as a piece of bark-fiber cloth is rough to the touch, this person, I tell you, is like that.
an3.99:2.7 | S: Any robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick that they receive are not very fruitful or beneficial for the donor. | T: “For those from whom he receives robes, alms food, lodging, & medicinal requisites for curing the sick, there is no great fruit, no great reward.
an3.99:2.8 | S: This is how they’re worthless, I say. | T: That, I tell you, is his being of next-to-no value.

