## an4.102
an4.102:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these four kinds of clouds. | T: “There are these four types of thunderheads.
an4.102:1.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an4.102:1.3 | S: One thunders but doesn’t rain, | T: One that thunders but doesn’t rain,
an4.102:1.4 | S: one rains but doesn’t thunder, | T: one that rains but doesn’t thunder,
an4.102:1.5 | S: one neither thunders nor rains, and | T: one that neither thunders nor rains,
an4.102:1.6 | S: one both rains and thunders. | T: and one that both thunders and rains.
an4.102:1.7 | S: These are the four kinds of clouds. | T: There are these four types of thunderheads.
an4.102:1.8 | S: In the same way, these four individuals similar to clouds are found in the world. | T: “In the same way, these four types of persons resembling thunderheads are to be found existing in the world.
an4.102:1.9 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an4.102:1.10 | S: One thunders but doesn’t rain, | T: The one who thunders but doesn’t rain,
an4.102:1.11 | S: one rains but doesn’t thunder, | T: the one who rains but doesn’t thunder,
an4.102:1.12 | S: one neither thunders nor rains, and | T: the one who neither thunders nor rains,
an4.102:1.13 | S: one both rains and thunders. | T: and the one who both thunders and rains.
an4.102:2.1 | S: And how does an individual thunder but not rain? | T: “And how is one the type of person who thunders but doesn’t rain?
an4.102:2.2 | S: It’s when an individual memorizes the teaching— | T: There is the case where a person has mastered the Dhamma:
an4.102:2.3 | S: statements, mixed prose & verse, discussions, verses, inspired exclamations, legends, stories of past lives, amazing stories, and elaborations. | T: dialogues, narratives of mixed prose and verse, explanations, verses, spontaneous exclamations [udāna], quotations [itivuttaka], birth stories [jātaka], amazing events, question & answer sessions.
an4.102:2.4 | S: But they don’t truly understand: ‘This is suffering’ … ‘This is the origin of suffering’ … ‘This is the cessation of suffering’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering’. | T: Yet he doesn’t discern, as it has come to be, that ‘This is stress.’ He doesn’t discern, as it has come to be, that ‘This is the origination of stress.’ He doesn’t discern, as it has come to be, that ‘This is the cessation of stress.’ He doesn’t discern, as it has come to be, that ‘This is the path of practice leading to the cessation of stress.’
an4.102:2.5 | S: That’s how an individual thunders but doesn’t rain. | T: This is the type of person who thunders but doesn’t rain.
an4.102:2.6 | S: That individual is like a cloud that thunders but doesn’t rain, I say. | T: This type of person, I tell you, is like the thunderhead that thunders but doesn’t rain.
an4.102:2.7 | P: tathūpamāhaṁ, bhikkhave, imaṁ puggalaṁ vadāmi. | T: (none)
an4.102:3.1 | S: And how does an individual rain but not thunder? | T: “And how is one the type of person who rains but doesn’t thunder?
an4.102:3.2 | S: It’s when an individual doesn’t memorize the teaching … | T: There is the case where a person has not mastered the Dhamma:
an4.102:3.3 | P: suttaṁ, geyyaṁ, veyyākaraṇaṁ, gāthaṁ, udānaṁ, itivuttakaṁ, jātakaṁ, abbhutadhammaṁ, vedallaṁ. | T: dialogues… question & answer sessions.
an4.102:3.4 | S: But they truly understand: ‘This is suffering’ … | T: Yet he does discern, as it has come to be, that ‘This is stress.’ He discerns, as it has come to be, that ‘This is the origination of stress.’ He discerns, as it has come to be, that ‘This is the cessation of stress.’ He discerns, as it has come to be, that ‘This is the path of practice leading to the cessation of stress.’
an4.102:3.5 | P: Evaṁ kho, bhikkhave, puggalo vassitā hoti, no gajjitā. | T: This is the type of person who rains but doesn’t thunder.
an4.102:3.6 | P: Seyyathāpi so, bhikkhave, valāhako vassitā, no gajjitā; | T: (none)
an4.102:3.7 | P: tathūpamāhaṁ, bhikkhave, imaṁ puggalaṁ vadāmi. | T: This type of person, I tell you, is like the thunderhead that rains but doesn’t thunder.
an4.102:4.1 | S: And how does an individual neither thunder nor rain? | T: “And how is one the type of person who neither thunders nor rains?
an4.102:4.2 | S: It’s when an individual doesn’t memorize the teaching … | T: There is the case where a person has not mastered the Dhamma:
an4.102:4.3 | P: suttaṁ, geyyaṁ, veyyākaraṇaṁ, gāthaṁ, udānaṁ, itivuttakaṁ, jātakaṁ, abbhutadhammaṁ, vedallaṁ. | T: dialogues… question & answer sessions.
an4.102:4.4 | S: Nor do they truly understand: ‘This is suffering’ … | T: He doesn’t discern, as it has come to be, that ‘This is stress’ … ‘This is the origination of stress’ … ‘This is the cessation of stress’ … ‘This is the path of practice leading to the cessation of stress.’
an4.102:4.5 | P: Evaṁ kho, bhikkhave, puggalo neva gajjitā hoti, no vassitā. | T: This is the type of person who neither thunders nor rains.
an4.102:4.6 | P: Seyyathāpi so, bhikkhave, valāhako neva gajjitā, no vassitā; | T: (none)
an4.102:4.7 | P: tathūpamāhaṁ, bhikkhave, imaṁ puggalaṁ vadāmi. | T: This type of person, I tell you, is like the thunderhead that neither thunders nor rains.
an4.102:5.1 | S: And how does an individual both thunder and rain? | T: “And how is one the type of person who both thunders and rains?
an4.102:5.2 | S: It’s when an individual memorizes the teaching … | T: There is the case where a person has mastered the Dhamma:
an4.102:5.3 | P: suttaṁ, geyyaṁ, veyyākaraṇaṁ, gāthaṁ, udānaṁ, itivuttakaṁ, jātakaṁ, abbhutadhammaṁ, vedallaṁ. | T: dialogues … question & answer sessions.
an4.102:5.4 | S: And they truly understand: ‘This is suffering’ … | T: He discerns, as it has come to be, that ‘This is stress’ … ‘This is the origination of stress’ … ‘This is the cessation of stress’ … ‘This is the path of practice leading to the cessation of stress.’
an4.102:5.5 | P: Evaṁ kho, bhikkhave, puggalo gajjitā ca hoti vassitā ca. | T: This is the type of person who both thunders and rains.
an4.102:5.6 | P: Seyyathāpi so, bhikkhave, valāhako gajjitā ca vassitā ca; | T: (none)
an4.102:5.7 | P: tathūpamāhaṁ, bhikkhave, imaṁ puggalaṁ vadāmi. | T: This type of person, I tell you, is like the thunderhead that both thunders and rains.
an4.102:5.8 | S: These four individuals similar to clouds are found in the world.” | T: “These are the four types of people to be found existing in the world.”
an4.102:5.9 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.111
an4.111:1.1 | S: Then Kesi the horse trainer went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to him, | T: Then Kesin the horse trainer went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down, sat to one side. As he was sitting there, the Blessed One said to him:
an4.111:1.2 | S: “Kesi, you’re known as a horse trainer. | T: “You, Kesin, are well known as a trainer of tamable horses.
an4.111:1.3 | S: Just how do you guide a horse in training?” | T: How do you train a tamable horse?”
an4.111:1.4 | S: “Sir, I guide a horse in training sometimes gently, sometimes harshly, and sometimes both gently and harshly.” | T: “Lord, I train a tamable horse (sometimes) with mildness, (sometimes) with harshness, (sometimes) with both mildness & harshness.”
an4.111:1.5 | S: “Kesi, what do you do with a horse in training that doesn’t follow these forms of training?” | T: “And if a tamable horse doesn’t submit either to a mild training or to a harsh training or to a mild & harsh training, Kesin, what do you do?”
an4.111:1.6 | P: “Sace me, bhante, assadammo saṇhena vinayaṁ na upeti, pharusena vinayaṁ na upeti, saṇhapharusena vinayaṁ na upeti; | T: “If a tamable horse doesn’t submit either to a mild training or to a harsh training or to a mild and harsh training,
an4.111:1.7 | S: “In that case, sir, I kill it. | T: lord, then I kill it.
an4.111:1.8 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that? (I think:)
an4.111:1.9 | S: So that I don’t disgrace my tutor’s community. | T: ‘Don’t let this be a disgrace to my lineage of teachers.’
an4.111:2.1 | S: But sir, the Buddha is the supreme guide for those fit for training. | T: But the Blessed One, lord, is the unexcelled trainer of tamable people.
an4.111:2.2 | S: Just how do you guide a person in training?” | T: How do you train a tamable person?”
an4.111:2.3 | S: “Kesi, I guide a person in training sometimes gently, sometimes harshly, and sometimes both gently and harshly. | T: “Kesin, I train a tamable person (sometimes) with mildness, (sometimes) with harshness, (sometimes) with both mildness & harshness.
an4.111:2.4 | S: The gentle way is this: | T: “In using mildness, (I teach:)
an4.111:2.5 | S: ‘This is good conduct by way of body, speech, and mind. This is the result of good conduct by way of body, speech, and mind. This is life as a god. This is life as a human.’ | T: ‘Such is good bodily conduct. Such is the result of good bodily conduct. Such is good verbal conduct. Such is the result of good verbal conduct. Such is good mental conduct. Such is the result of good mental conduct. Such are the devas. Such are human beings.’
an4.111:2.6 | S: The harsh way is this: | T: “In using harshness, (I teach:)
an4.111:2.7 | S: ‘This is bad conduct by way of body, speech, and mind. This is the result of bad conduct by way of body, speech, and mind. This is life in hell. This is life as an animal. This is life as a ghost.’ | T: ‘Such is bodily misconduct. Such is the result of bodily misconduct. Such is verbal misconduct. Such is the result of verbal misconduct. Such is mental misconduct. Such is the result of mental misconduct. Such is hell. Such is the animal womb. Such the realm of the hungry ghosts.’
an4.111:3.1 | S: The both gentle and harsh way is this: | T: “In using mildness & harshness, (I teach:)
an4.111:3.2 | S: ‘This is good conduct … this is bad conduct …’” | T: ‘Such is good bodily conduct. Such is the result of good bodily conduct. Such is bodily misconduct. Such is the result of bodily misconduct. Such is good verbal conduct. Such is the result of good verbal conduct. Such is verbal misconduct. Such is the result of verbal misconduct. Such is good mental conduct. Such is the result of good mental conduct. Such is mental misconduct. Such is the result of mental misconduct. Such are the devas. Such are human beings. Such is hell. Such is the animal womb. Such the realm of the hungry ghosts.’”
an4.111:4.1 | S: “Sir, what do you do with a person in training who doesn’t follow these forms of training?” | T: “And if a tamable person doesn’t submit either to a mild training or to a harsh training or to a mild & harsh training, what do you do?”
an4.111:4.2 | S: “In that case, Kesi, I kill them.” | T: “If a tamable person doesn’t submit either to a mild training or to a harsh training or to a mild & harsh training, then I kill him, Kesin.”
an4.111:4.3 | S: “Sir, it’s not appropriate for the Buddha to kill living creatures. | T: “But it’s not proper for our Blessed One to take life!
an4.111:4.4 | S: And yet you say | T: And yet the Blessed One just said,
an4.111:4.5 | S: you kill them.” | T: ‘I kill him, Kesin.’”
an4.111:4.6 | S: “It’s true, Kesi, | T: “It is true, Kesin,
an4.111:4.7 | S: it’s not appropriate for a Realized One to kill living creatures. | T: that it’s not proper for a Tathāgata to take life.
an4.111:4.8 | S: But when a person in training doesn’t follow any of these forms of training, the Realized One doesn’t think they’re worth advising or instructing, and neither do their sensible spiritual companions. | T: But if a tamable person doesn’t submit either to a mild training or to a harsh training or to a mild & harsh training, then the Tathāgata doesn’t regard him as being worth speaking to or admonishing. His observant companions in the holy life don’t regard him as being worth speaking to or admonishing.
an4.111:4.9 | S: For it is killing in the training of the Noble One | T: This is what it means to be totally destroyed in the Dhamma & Vinaya,
an4.111:4.10 | S: when the Realized One doesn’t think they’re worth advising or instructing, and neither do their sensible spiritual companions.” | T: when the Tathāgata doesn’t regard one as being worth speaking to or admonishing, and one’s observant companions in the holy life don’t regard one as being worth speaking to or admonishing.”
an4.111:5.1 | S: “Well, they’re definitely dead | T: “Yes, lord, wouldn’t one be totally destroyed
an4.111:5.2 | S: when the Realized One doesn’t think they’re worth advising or instructing, and neither do their sensible spiritual companions. | T: if the Tathāgata doesn’t regard one as being worth speaking to or admonishing, and one’s observant companions in the holy life don’t regard one as being worth speaking to or admonishing?
an4.111:5.3 | S: Excellent, sir! … From this day forth, may the Buddha remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge for life.” | T: “Magnificent, lord! Magnificent! Just as if he were to place upright what was overturned, to reveal what was hidden, to show the way to one who was lost, or to carry a lamp into the dark so that those with eyes could see forms, in the same way has the Blessed One—through many lines of reasoning—made the Dhamma clear. I go to the Blessed One for refuge, to the Dhamma, and to the Saṅgha of monks. May the Blessed One remember me as a lay follower who has gone to him for refuge, from this day forward, for life.”
an4.111:5.4 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)


